= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Multiply by a <test function> vanishing at the endpoints and apply <integration by parts>. The positive operator is $-u''+xu$, so the forcing changes sign. The <weak formulation> of this <forced Airy boundary-value problem> is: find $u\in V=H_0^1(0,1)$ such that
$$
\boxed{a(u,v)=\ell(v)\quad(v\in V),\qquad
a(u,v)=\int_0^1(u'v'+xuv)\,dx,\quad
\ell(v)=-\int_0^1v\,dx.}
$$
Here $V$ is the <zero-boundary Sobolev space> and can be equipped with $\|v\|_V=\|v'\|_{L^2}$. The interval <Poincare inequality> states $\|v\|_{L^2}\leq\pi^{-1}\|v'\|_{L^2}$, so this <norm> makes $V$ a <Hilbert space> and is equivalent to its usual $H^1$ <Sobolev norm>. The form is symmetric and satisfies
$$
|a(u,v)|\leq(1+\pi^{-2})\|u\|_V\|v\|_V,\qquad
a(v,v)\geq\|v\|_V^2,\qquad
|\ell(v)|\leq\pi^{-1}\|v\|_V.
$$
The <Lax-Milgram theorem> says that a bounded <coercive bilinear form> on a <Hilbert space> and a <bounded linear functional> determine exactly one weak solution. All its hypotheses have just been verified. Moreover, $u''=xu+1\in L^2$ gives $u\in H^2$ on the interval; the right-hand side is then continuous, so this weak solution is the classical solution with the prescribed endpoint values.

Since the form is symmetric, the equivalent <Ritz method> minimizes
$$
\boxed{J(v)=\frac12\int_0^1[(v')^2+xv^2]\,dx+\int_0^1v\,dx
\quad\text{over }H_0^1(0,1).}
$$
Indeed, stationarity gives the weak equation and
$$
J(v)-J(u)=\tfrac12a(v-u,v-u)\geq0,
$$
with equality only at $u$. This proves the minimization equivalence, including the sign of the linear term.

Restricting to a <conforming finite element space> $V_h\subset V$ gives a unique discrete solution and <Galerkin orthogonality>, $a(u-u_h,v_h)=0$ for every $v_h\in V_h$. The <Céa lemma> states that if the continuity and coercivity constants are $M$ and $\alpha$, then $\|u-u_h\|_V\leq(M/\alpha)\inf_{v_h\in V_h}\|u-v_h\|_V$. For this symmetric problem the exact best-approximation constant in the <energy norm> is one. The piecewise-linear <finite element interpolation estimate> $\|u-I_hu\|_{H^1}\leq Ch\|u\|_{H^2}$ on regular <finite element meshes> therefore gives <convergence of a numerical method> of the <Ritz method>, with first-order error in the <energy norm>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take a uniform <finite element mesh> $x_i=ih$, $0\leq i\leq J$, $h=1/J$, and interior <piecewise-linear hat functions> $\phi_i$. Write $u_h=\sum_{i=1}^{J-1}U_i\phi_i$. The <Ritz method> gives $KU=F$ with
$$
K_{ij}=\int_0^1\phi_i'\phi_j'\,dx+\int_0^1x\phi_i\phi_j\,dx,
\qquad F_i=-\int_0^1\phi_i\,dx=-h.
$$
The derivative part has diagonal $2/h$, neighboring entries $-1/h$ and zero other entries. For the <affine-weighted hat mass matrix>, symmetry around $x_i$ gives
$$
\int x\phi_i^2\,dx=\frac{2h}{3}x_i.
$$
On $[x_i,x_{i+1}]$, the product of the two hats is symmetric about the midpoint, integrates to $h/6$, and therefore has weighted integral
$$
\int x\phi_i\phi_{i+1}\,dx=\frac h{12}(x_i+x_{i+1}).
$$
Consequently the explicit <tridiagonal> equations are
$$
\boxed{\left[-\frac1h+\frac h{12}(x_{i-1}+x_i)\right]U_{i-1}
+\left[\frac2h+\frac{2h}3x_i\right]U_i
+\left[-\frac1h+\frac h{12}(x_i+x_{i+1})\right]U_{i+1}=-h,}
$$
for $1\leq i\leq J-1$, with $U_0=U_J=0$. The reaction coefficient has been integrated exactly; replacing it by an unweighted constant <mass matrix> would give a different system. For a nonzero coefficient <vector>, the represented function is nonzero and $U^TKU=a(u_h,u_h)>0$, so the <matrix> is symmetric <positive-definite> and the algebraic solution is unique.

Uniform spacing is a choice, not a requirement of the method. On a general <finite element mesh> with $h_i=x_i-x_{i-1}$, the corresponding entries are
$$
K_{ii}=\frac1{h_i}+\frac1{h_{i+1}}
+\frac{h_i}{12}(x_{i-1}+3x_i)
+\frac{h_{i+1}}{12}(3x_i+x_{i+1}),
$$
$$
K_{i,i+1}=-\frac1{h_{i+1}}+
\frac{h_{i+1}}{12}(x_i+x_{i+1}),\qquad
F_i=-\frac{h_i+h_{i+1}}2,
$$
with symmetric lower entries. These reduce to the displayed uniform-grid system.

